    {
      "type": "proposition",
      "content": "There are a number of ways to parameterize the general two-biallelic-locus fitness model ([[SEE_TABLE:5.2]]). Under the assumption of no cis/trans effects, there are eight free parameters (one of the nine fitnesses can always be normalized to one). When fitnesses are additive across loci (i.e., no epistasis but the possibility of dominance at each locus), two-locus systems (or multi-locus systems for that matter) will be well behaved in that there is at most one polymorphic equilibrium for any given set of segregating alleles, at which $ \\overline{W} $ is at a local maximum (Karlin and Liberman 1979). While D is zero at equilibrium in such cases, selection generates non-zero D during the sojourn to the equilibrium value for the gamete frequencies.",
      "source_page": 128,
      "source_pages": [
        128,
        129
      ],
